The coverage selection is label-based, not outcome-based: require all four covered classes, at least five cyclists, zero uncovered objects, then minimize GT count and lexical identity. That is sensible for a first implementation gate, but selects away the very assignment failures present in balanced16. Passing this chosen frame should not imply passing a representative scene distribution. [Selection implementation](../tier1/catalog.py), [fixture receipt](tier1-allclass-fixture-verified.json).

The historical balanced16 receipts' step records independently show exactly **125 visits to every frame** at 2,000 updates; the single-frame baseline first passes at 500 visits and confirms at 750. Thus “2,000 failed while 750 passed” compares different exposure per frame as well as different scene diversity. Equalizing visits would mean 8,000/12,000 total updates for sixteen frames; it would still not remove multi-frame interference, class/support differences, or inference differences. The new sustained loop uses `step % 16` round robin, whereas the historical worker uses seeded permutations. A new sustained run is a new trajectory, not an exact continuation of the historical 2,000-step shuffled curve. [Historical worker](../cohort/train_balanced.py), [historical complete records](balanced-study-recovery-20261002.json), [sustained loop](../cohort/sustained_loop.py).

## What “one factor” means in these recipes

The original catalog fixes GN8 in the BEV blocks and upsamplers **with pillar BN retained**, 32 points/pillar, 20,000-pillar cap, Adam 1e-4, betas .9/.999, epsilon 1e-8, no weight decay, `foreach=False`, clip 10, reference loss, no special foreground prior. Common weights are constructed before substitutions; the live treatment contract checks exact compatible convolution, pillar-linear and head initialization. Seed 17, deterministic FP32, TF32 off, identical targets and decoder make these substantially cleaner comparisons than unrelated training runs. A common seed is one deterministic trial, not an uncertainty estimate. [Catalog](../tier1/catalog.py), [model/objective/optimizer](../tier1/models.py), [live treatment checks](../tier1/treatment_contract.py), [closed result](tier1-overfit20261002b-closed.json).

Several “architecture” treatments deliberately bundle mechanisms:

- `deep_pfn` adds a 64→64 bias-free linear layer **and point-channel LN**; its extra 4,224 parameters are only about 0.087% of the 4,847,144-parameter baseline. This is a modest PFN-depth treatment, not a test of substantially larger model capacity.
- `context_pfn` adds max/mean context, a projection and LN, and masks pooled support. Compare it with `masked_pfn` to avoid attributing a padding-policy effect solely to context.
- `masked_pfn` masks padded positions before final max but leaves padded slots in BN. It does not test masked normalization.
- `residual_bev` changes skip connectivity with the same parameter count. It is a useful matched structural comparison.
- `window_bev` versus `coarse_mlp` has the same 5,109,800 parameters, but their attention/position processing and nonlinearities differ; it is a concrete recipe pair, not a universally isolated “attention” primitive.
- Fine/coarse grouping changes cell size **and first convolution stride** to retain the same 0.5 m output-anchor spacing. Therefore the fine-grid result is not evidence about finer detection-head spacing or better anchor coverage: targets remain unchanged.
- Ragged pillars retain every ROI point in selected pillars and eliminate padded slots from BN/reduction. Its effect includes measurement retention, cluster means, normalization population and pooling layout. It does not isolate the 32-point cutoff alone.
- Sparse BEV replaces the dense spatial backbone with occupied-token pooling/attention and LN. It has **2,064,808 parameters**, versus 4,847,144 for the dense baseline; capacity, sparsity, aggregation and normalization all differ. Its failure does not reject transformers as a family or constitute an SWFormer reproduction.

Sources: [PFN/residual variants](../gpu/architecture_variants.py), [masked/window/MLP variants](../gpu/architecture_followups.py), [advanced factory](../advanced/models.py), [ragged/point modules](../advanced/point_modules.py), [grid/sparse backbone](../advanced/spatial_modules.py), [actual parameter/result counts](advanced-expanded20261002a-results.json).

Grouping also changes real observation coverage. On this frame there are 159,624 ROI points: baseline retains 98,070; retain64 116,207; fine grid 79,758; coarse grid 68,974; ragged 159,624. Fine grouping creates 30,834 eligible pillars and hits the 20,000 cap, dropping 57,968 points through pillar selection plus 21,898 through the point cutoff. Baseline has only 15,079 pillars, so `all_pillars` is exactly equivalent and supplies no additional independently trained winner. Every tested grouping retains at least one measured point in each of the 73 eligible boxes; this does not imply equivalent geometric information. [Baseline/64/cap lineage and support](tier1-allclass-fixture-verified.json), [advanced grouping lineage and support](advanced-grouping-fixture-verified.json).

## Numerical signals and their limits

The acceptance rule for the corrected sweep is native LEVEL2 APH ≥ .8 **in every class** at consecutive sampled checkpoints, including terminal. Most architecture recipes and LR/class weighting/LN controls first pass at 500 and confirm at 750; their crossing is bounded by the samples `(300, 500]`, not measured exactly. Ranking them by near-saturated terminal scores hides the earlier trajectory and differing terminal training lengths. Shared GPU contention is documented, and synchronized timing still includes contention; these times are not a reliable architecture speed leaderboard. [Sweep report](tier1-overfit20261002b-results.md), [complete curves](tier1-overfit20261002b-results.json), [expanded curves](advanced-expanded20261002a-results.json).

| Treatment | First observed pass | Confirmation | Interpretation on this frame |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Baseline; deep/context/masked PFN; residual/window/MLP BEV; retain64 | 500 | 750 | All fit; sampled brackets do not identify a winner. |
| Pillar LN with GN BEV; LR 3e-4; class-balanced positive focal | 500 | 750 | All fit; mechanisms produce different class trajectories. |
| Foreground bias prior .01 | 750 | 1,000 | Better early rare-class scores, but later all-class gate than baseline. |
| Full BN | 2,000 | 3,000 | Large early train/eval discrepancy and later inference fit. |
| No clipping | 3,000 | 4,000 | Slower fitting under the unchanged Adam/LR recipe. |
| No normalization | 6,000 | 8,000 | Can fit, but difficult optimization under this exact bias-free recipe. |
| Expanded grid/ragged/point/range recipes and controls | 500 | 750 | Seven recipes fit; mechanism pairs have meaningful score differences below. |
| Sparse BEV transformer | None through 10,000 | None | Negative recipe result: terminal vehicle APH .741925; remaining classes .990472/.926840/.998926. |

Sources: [original fit intervals](tier1-overfit20261002b-results.json), [expanded fit intervals](advanced-expanded20261002a-results.json). The adopted baseline trajectory continued to 4,000 before the decoder issue was resolved; use its 500/750 values for matched early comparisons, rather than comparing its 4,000 terminal with another recipe's 750 terminal.

**Normalization has stronger evidence than a generic size hypothesis.** At full-BN step 200, eval total loss is 23.6361 versus batch-statistics loss .535214; step 500 gives 2.00955 versus .0644306. The pillar-LN/GN treatment has literally identical complete train/eval loss dictionaries at all eight saved checkpoints, verified directly from the result JSON. Baseline still has pillar BN, so calling it “BN-free” is inaccurate. No norm removes affine parameters and keeps the existing bias-free layers; it clips 7,978/8,000 updates (99.725%), compared with much lower clipping counts in the normal treatments. This supports using the working GN recipe for diagnosis, but does not identify the separate effects of activation conditioning, effective update size and moving statistics. [Norm implementation](../gpu/norm_variants.py), [full-BN/LN/no-norm curves](tier1-overfit20261002b-results.json).

The normalization gap also appears on the actual harder **sixteen-frame** cohort. Recomputing the mean of the sixteen `component_curve` records at step 2,000 gives:

| Candidate | Eval total | Buffer-restored batch-statistics total | Eval/batch-statistics ratio | Eval localization | Batch-statistics localization | Localization ratio |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Baseline | 1.073229872 | .294972289 | 3.638409 | .324763148 | .049392819 | 6.575109 |
| Residual BEV | 1.266910361 | .294759648 | 4.298113 | .381633279 | .050953541 | 7.489828 |

These are same-weight loss observations under eval versus training-mode statistics, with normalization buffers restored after the diagnostic forward. They are **not native quality rescue results**: the records do not supply independently scored sixteen-frame batch-statistics predictions. They make a frozen-weight, fixed16 inference-normalization diagnostic and a matched pillar-LN candidate higher-information next investigations than assuming inadequate feature capacity. Keep the native GT and decoder fixed, measure actual AP/APH and per-object localization, and preserve the baseline weights and buffers as a separate control. A smaller diagnostic loss alone cannot show that all-class detection quality is repaired. [All sixteen terminal component records](balanced-study-recovery-20261002.json), [buffer restoration and diagnostic forward](../cohort/train_balanced.py), [pillar normalization axis](../gpu/norm_variants.py).

A stronger causal intervention already exists on the older 17-object frame: fixed 2,000-step weights, zero optimizer updates, one physical-only BN recalibration changes total loss **.05814297→.00748348** and historical mean APH **.73752967→.864156**; sign APH changes .226459→.595564. The receipt establishes bitwise unchanged learned weights and independent export/native replay. It proves that BN buffers affected that frame's inference. It does not pass the current all-class gate: no cyclists, sign APH remains below .8, and the old mean gate averages only populated classes. [Frozen-weight intervention](one-batch-bn-counterfactual-verified.json), [before native scores](native-one-batch-overfit-scoring-verified.json), [after native scores](one-batch-bn-counterfactual-scoring-verified.json), [independent replay](one-batch-bn-counterfactual-score-audits-verified.json).

**Class weighting changes which class learns first.** At step 300, baseline APH is V/P/S/C = **.887463/.730200/.462649/0**, versus **.468252/.997111/.864534/.996689** with class-balanced positive focal. By step 500 both pass every class. The intervention weights only true-class positive focal terms; it does not globally reweight background or localization, or repair missing assignments. Given 338/30/16/6 positive anchors, weights are about .2885/3.25/6.0938/16.25. This is a useful rare-class priority signal, with a vehicle tradeoff; it is not faster all-class fitting on this sampled grid. [Exact objective](../tier1/models.py), [class curves](tier1-overfit20261002b-results.json).

**Low prior is promising mechanistically, not a confirmed faster learner.** The .01 bias sharply reduces initial background focal loss and gives cyclist APH .488146 at step 100 and .792962 at 200, where baseline remains zero. Yet sign APH is .789220 at 500, delaying all-class acceptance to 750/1,000. Lower initial loss and higher early foreground scores do not by themselves identify improved localization or final generalization. [Prior initialization](../tier1/models.py), [prior and baseline curves](tier1-overfit20261002b-results.json).

**Mechanism controls should be read as pairs, not collapsed into “all equal.”** Point attention and point MLP both have 4,880,552 parameters and pass at 500/750; at terminal 750 their vehicle/sign APH is **.984122/.926812** versus **.880378/.811483**. This is a real single-trial difference worth investigating, but the shared fit bracket and single frame do not establish reliable attention benefit. Range fusion and its zero-range control both have 5,321,352 parameters and the same fit bracket; terminal vehicle APH is **.946316 versus .999391**, so this experiment does not show range-feature benefit. The zero control still computes the range branch and then zeroes gathered features, making it a compute-matched measurement-input control. The fusion projection is zero-initialized, giving exact initial baseline heads. Measure branch gradients and gate magnitude before attributing success to range information. [Point modules](../advanced/point_modules.py), [range implementation](../advanced/range_fusion.py), [initial equality contract](../advanced/model_contract.py), [paired curves](advanced-expanded20261002a-results.json).

## Evaluation corrections matter as much as architecture changes

The V3 yaw decoder first reduces angles into the canonical period before applying the direction-bin branch. A paired test on identical saved single-frame heads improved pedestrian APH about .759→1.000 and sign .714→.928 without training or AP changes. That isolates an evaluation defect, not an architecture gain. The remaining sign AP near 13/14 follows a suppressed overlapping pair under the fixed NMS policy. [Paired heading evaluation code](../tier1/rescore_heading.py), [branch diagnostics](tier1-heading-branch-diagnostics.json), [decoder implementation](../gpu/scored_proposals_v3.py), [corrected sweep explanation](tier1-overfit20261002b-results.md).

`scoring-parallel-v3` in historical balanced16 filenames names orchestration, not yaw decoder V3: its preparation worker imports `scored_proposals_v2`. Historical baseline/residual APH at 2,000 is .306981/.237434/.0225062/.00207839 and .266018/.154996/.002694/.004545 respectively. Their AP values are also all below .8, so a heading-only repair cannot turn those outputs into accepted fitting. [Historical preparation](../cohort/prepare_v2.py), [historical scores](balanced-study-recovery-20261002.json).

The newer unchanged baseline-2000 V3/full-GT rescore reports APH **.281619/.242956/.0236408/.00190988** and AP **.377241/.275674/.0274796/.0019305**. Its GT policy is all 1,279 native four-class boxes (639/300/302/38), with the evaluator deciding metric eligibility; the same training targets still contain 1,053 ROI-eligible boxes. This supersedes the statement that baseline corrected APH has never been rerun, while preserving the historical numbers. A matched residual V3/full-GT rescore is not established by the records inspected here. [Metric receipt](balanced16-historical-v3-fullgt-metrics-verified.json), [exact native/export replay](balanced16-historical-v3-native-metric-audit-verified.json), [literal proposal audit](balanced16-historical-v3-proposal-audit-verified.json), [original-head export binding](balanced16-historical-v3-fullgt-export-verified.json).

Receipt SHA256 identities for this newer result are respectively `6a59512f16a9cbc927f53f71f0cb75470e3e5dfe8bd62c882b0329c590cb0f50`, `f2ca241fc6db6a7f82e73f4523d326aea77c7d4e591cf2aeb27693c3b4d6f594`, `db1b767e5565eb3a107aca97bc080e88a5052bb461bc6e6707e5076a2b3fb2b3`, and `92bae9921d46e5fd57dd7458eb0b845fb520fc8bf41d5db33bfabcbc7ed7ab0e`. All four receipts record exit code zero.

The ideal covered-target diagnostic reaches only **.759399 sign APH** against full native GT, whereas ideal ROI targets reach .868421 and all positive-point native GT reaches 1. There are 29 uncovered signs and one uncovered pedestrian. Literal assignment reconstruction shows 28 strict higher-overlap conflicts and two ties; 28 conflicts are within the same class. Simple class-restricted matching cannot resolve those same-class collisions. This is a measured coverage weakness independent of encoder size, but not a mathematical upper bound on a detector that can predict unsupervised objects. [Native oracle and independent replay](balanced16-coverage-oracle-native-verified.json), [oracle audit](balanced16-coverage-oracle-audit-verified.json), [per-object literal cause audit](balanced16-coverage-causes-verified.json), [interpretation](balanced16-coverage-oracle-status.md).

## Measurements that can select a direction

These are proposed discriminating investigations, not completed experiments or new architecture implementations. Each should preserve a fixed manifest, weights, decoder, GT policy and native receipt chain; annotation-derived oracle interventions must remain diagnostics rather than model inputs or reported learned predictions.

| Question | High-information measurement | Signal and decision it enables |
|---|---|---|
| Is failure primarily support or assignment? | One row per native GT: full/retained point counts, ROI status, maximum anchor overlap, positive count, competing GT and class, z separation, predicted recall. Stratify covered/uncovered and small/large objects. | Absence of training assignments calls for a separate assignment/geometry control; low retained physical support calls for grouping/support investigation. Neither warrants increasing all module widths by default. |
| Are boxes accurate but badly ranked? | On frozen baseline heads, compare class/score-only oracle intervention with unchanged boxes against the original predictions, before and after NMS. Record per-class foreground score distributions and GT best-box IoU. | Large score-only rescue with good boxes directs effort to classification/weighting/ranking; little rescue with poor IoU directs effort to localization/representation. |
| Does suppression remove already learned objects? | Trace each matched GT through score floor, global pre-4096 rank, NMS and post-500 truncation. Compare fixed-head class-wise/height-aware or geometric suppression only as explicit counterfactual diagnostics. | A GT present before NMS and absent after it identifies inference loss; an absent pre-NMS match cannot be repaired by NMS alone. |
| Are BN buffers hiding learned quality? | On identical frozen weights and unchanged data, compare eval outputs with buffer-restored batch-statistics outputs and a training-only recalibration control. Score every class through the same current decoder/GT. | A reproducible frozen-weight quality rescue isolates normalization state. A small gap leaves optimization/representation as the stronger focus. Preserve an independent baseline and avoid held-out calibration leakage. |
| Is sixteen-frame fitting just underexposed or suffering interference? | Report updates **and visits per frame**, per-frame/per-class curves, probability/IoU distributions, and fixed-order sampler identity. Compare the selected frame alone with the same frame during cohort training at matched visits. | Improved quality after exposure matching favors a budget explanation; regression on a previously learned frame after other-frame updates points toward interference, support imbalance or normalization drift. Update equality alone cannot discriminate these. |
| Does reweighting solve class learning? | On the fixed16 matched recipes, report positive-class gradient contributions before clipping, background gradients, clipping multiplier, and per-object coverage alongside native AP/APH. | Rare classes gain ranking/localization without removing targets or sacrificing vehicles: carry the recipe to the next tier. A low total loss or uncovered-sign failure alone is not a verdict on weighting. |
| Are attention/range gains repeatable and useful? | First dissect matched point-attention/MLP and range/zero pairs at the same checkpoints and GT; inspect branch gradients, learned fusion magnitudes, failed object IDs and heading/AP differences. Then repeat shortlisted pairs across fixed training cohorts/seeds and separate held-out scenes. | A consistent native quality/resource benefit in the claimed regime supports advancement. One near-saturated frame, or an inactive fusion branch, supports only feasibility. |

The original fixed-frame suite is a valuable first gate: it catches incapable recipes and verifies full loss/export/evaluation plumbing. To choose a research direction, attach object-level failure attribution and matched exposure to the fixed16 tier, then require held-out evidence. The current practical defaults remain the functioning modest pillar/dense-GN model and retained clipping. First investigate the measured fixed16 normalization gap through frozen-weight native scoring and a matched pillar-LN candidate; class weighting and prior controls also deserve investigation because their effects are visible in class learning curves. Point attention warrants a controlled follow-up because its paired terminal evidence is positive on this frame. Range fusion needs evidence of actual useful branch contribution before promotion. The sparse recipe remains a recorded negative candidate rather than a rejected research family.
